    "evidence_text": "dialogue agents have met significant difficulties, such as maintaining conversation consistency .\n\nMany agents use personality models that attempt to map personality attributes into lower dimensional spaces (e.g. the Big Five ).\n\nHowever, these represent human personality at a very high-level and lack depth.\n\nThey prohibit the ability to link specific and detailed personality traits to characters, and to construct large datasets where dialogue is traceable back to these traits.\n\nSee Figure FIGREF1 for an example.\n\nHuman Attributes) as a novel method of incorporating HLAs into dialogue agents.\n\nWe also run a human evaluation supporting our results.\n\nWe demonstrate that ALOHA, combined with the proposed dataset, outperforms baselines.\n\nWe plan to release all of ALOHA's data and code.\n\nExamples include the\n\nstrong representation of human dialogue in reality.\n\nSimilar to Wikipedia, its content is provided and edited collaboratively by a massive user-base.\n\nWe use tropes as HLAs to calculate correlations with specific target characters.\n\nWe collect data from numerous characters from a variety of TV shows, movies, and anime.\n\nEach collected character has 20.64 HLAs on average.\n\nSee Figure FIGREF1 for an example character and their HLAs.\n\nIt is a character specific language model refined from a general language model.\n\nOur system is also able to pick responses which are correct both in context as well as character space.\n\nFirst, given a set of characters, determine the character space using the CSM.\n\nNext, given a specific target character, determine the positive community and negative set of associated characters using the CCM.\n\nMethodology ::: Character Space Module (CSM)\tCSM learns how to rank characters.\n\nWe can measure the interdependencies between the HLA variables  and rank the similarity between the TV show characters.\n\ndataset, and $P_{u,i} = 0$ otherwise.\n\nSee Equation DISPLAY_FORM8.\n\nWe find that using $\\alpha =20$ provides decent results.\n\nThe second term is an L2 regularizer to reduce overfitting.\n\nWe can then sample dialogue from characters in the negative set to act as\n\nHLAs.\n\nSee Figure FIGREF1 for an example of these dialogue lines.\n\nWe sampled characters from four different groups or regions.\n\nThe dialogue data used is discussed in Section SECREF15.\n\nSee Appendix C for five-fold cross validation details and statistics.\n\nEvaluation ::: Automatic Evaluation Setup ::: Five Evaluation Characters\tare chosen, one from each of the five testing\n\nsets above.\n\nEach is a well-known character from a separate TV show, and acts as a target character $c_t$ for evaluation of every model.\n\nWe choose well-known characters because humans require knowledge on the characters they are evaluating (see Section SECREF40).\n\nFor each of these five evaluation characters, all the dialogue lines from the character act as the ground truth responses.\n\nThe use of negative character sampling is only in ALOHA's training.\n\nEvaluation ::: Baselines",
